/* MONTAVERA_HISTORY_ACCOUNTS — the filed account of each day, verbatim from the
   month back pages ("Explanations") of The Fifty-Six, keyed by day of year.
   Source: the calendar build data behind the printed book (months/*.md, ## BACK).
   Days with no filed account are absent from this object, and the page says so. */
var MONTAVERA_HISTORY_ACCOUNTS = {"1": "Rent is due on the year's first morning, and the Deep City signup window opens beside the rent window, because the Bureau believes a citizen should begin the year already owing and already curious. Sign early. By the third day the list is full of people who looked down once and have thought about it since.","2": "The Nawn Coalition patrols the first stairs of the year, on foot, in silence, looking at everything with no eyes. Count the stairs as you pass them. They are counting you, and a citizen who counts along is considered courteous.","3": "The Thanking of the Stairs falls on the year's first Sunsday, and the Pseudo-Religious Service follows it directly, so gratitude and worship share one queue. Every stair in the city is thanked by name or by number. The stairs do not reply. The stairs are not required to reply. The thanking is for the citizen.","4": "The white moon is new over a new year: fresh pages, old ink. The Bureau prints the year's ledgers in January ink, which is to say last year's ink, warmed. Nothing in the city is wasted, including darkness.","6": "The first Howling Rites of the year are held early in Hendiber so the year can be brought in at the correct volume. Residents of adjoining streets are reminded that participation is voluntary and audibility is not.","7": "The Factions Tournament opens its season on the first Ghoulday of the year. Last year's grudges are carried forward automatically; new ones may be registered at the door. The Committee wishes everyone a clean game and files the wish under optimism.","9": "The Aria Distribution Allowance and Adopt a Rat Day share the 9th this year, and the queue at Window 2 is therefore long, mixed, and patient. Bring two forms: one for the Aria, one for the rat. Rats are issued by temperament, not by request. The rat you receive is the rat you were describing.","11": "Quarter moon, and half the month's ledger is still blank, which the senior clerks regard as the year's last moment of innocence. Fill it kindly. It will not stay blank, and it remembers who wrote in it first.","13": "The Elder Raven hears the year's first confessions on the 13th. It does not absolve. It files. Citizens who confessed in Oldber and again in Hendiber are reminded that the Raven keeps both copies and has noticed the differences.","17": "Other Skin Day falls on a Sunsday in Hendiber, and the service is held in whatever skin you were issued. The hymns are the same. The congregation is not. Return the skin by sundown, dry, with the form, and attend the evening queue as yourself, which by then you will be glad of.","18": "The moon is full and the pamphleteers of Sollen's Mouth open their year by agreeing on nothing, in writing, beautifully. Their first pamphlet of the year is traditionally a correction of their last pamphlet of the old year. Both are available at the door. Both are correct. Ask neither.","19": "The stairs are counted twice today, once by the district and once by the stairs' own auditor, whose identity is filed and whose count never matches. The discrepancy is the city's oldest small mystery and is renewed annually, like a subscription.","20": "The escorted visit to the Deep City departs at the hour on your ticket and returns one hour later, and the Howling Rites take the evening shift so the day is bracketed by descents of different kinds. The escort does not answer questions. You know this by now. It is only Hendiber, and already you know.","21": "The Factions Tournament shares the day with the Contest of Excellent Excuses, and the scheduling is deliberate: every player arrives with a prepared account of their last defeat. Excuses are judged on craft, plausibility, and posture. The winning excuse is entered into the player's permanent file, where it does the most good.","22": "Ratification Day. Everything provisionally approved last year must be signed again today, in the signer's own hand, with the signer's own doubts. What is not re-signed lapses quietly and is carried to the In-Transit House. Sign what you meant. Amend what you didn't. The pen is provided; the meaning is yours.","25": "Quarter moon. Citizens are invited to rehearse their gratitude before the year's thanksgivings multiply beyond choreographing. A short rehearsal, standing, in the direction of the stairs. No audience is required. The stairs are audience enough.","27": "The Day Everything Is Where It Was Left. Once a year the city wakes to find nothing moved, nothing borrowed, nothing filed under a neighbor's name, and this includes the streets: on this day the streets do NOT flip at sundown. It is the year's great stillness and the clerks' only holiday, taken standing still, in a city holding its breath. Enjoy it. Tomorrow the geography resumes.","28": "The first In-Transit House auction of the year follows the tournament, and the year's first unclaimed things change hands: lapsed approvals, unsigned intentions, one staircase (buyer collects). Bid in Teeth, in feathers, in Twists. Bid like someone whose name is on something in the back room, because it is.","29": "Rent is due, the Deep City signup window opens, and summer is filed open all on one morning. Sign up early and bring water for the queue. Citizens planning to descend in Sunmanber are advised that it is the coolest appointment in the city and the list knows it.","30": "In Sunmanber the suns rise before the clerks and stay up past the windows. Business continues as usual, only longer. The Bureau reminds citizens that extra daylight is not extra time; it is only extra light, and the deadlines have not moved an inch.","31": "The first Pseudo-Religious Service of the month is held outdoors, weather permitting, and in Sunmanber the weather is always permitting and usually attending. Bring a hymn and a hat. The suns are counted among the congregation and are not required to sing.","32": "The white moon is new, and in Sunmanber even the dark takes a holiday: the moonless night is short, bright at the edges, and full of other people's music. Sleep is available but unpopular. The Bureau does not schedule examinations by this moon.","34": "Other Skin Day, with the Howling Rites in the evening: you will spend the day as someone else and the night howling as yourself, and citizens consistently report that the order matters. Return the skin by sundown, dry, with the form. The howling requires no form. The howling IS the form.","35": "The Factions Tournament plays in the shade all month, under awnings sold by the honest yard, and the tables are rotated at noon so no player keeps the cool seat past fairness. The ice vendor referees the late games. The ice vendor has never been wrong, because by then nobody can feel the cards.","37": "The Aria Distribution Allowance is paid at Window 2. Bring two forms and a fan. The queue moves at the speed of summer, which is to say it ripens rather than advances, and the clerks fan themselves with rejected applications, which is permitted and even traditional.","39": "The Kerzit Pest Patrol Discount Extravaganza Day. Once a year the Patrol offers its rates at a discount so steep the Committee requires the word Extravaganza by law. Book your inspections, your clearings, your discreet removals. The kerzit are not consulted about the discount. The kerzit are the discount's occasion.","41": "The Elder Raven hears confessions through the hottest part of the year without complaint, which citizens find more unsettling than absolution anyway. It does not absolve. It files. In summer it also accepts iced water, which it does not file, and this exception is treasured.","44": "The Nawn Coalition patrols Sunmanber slowly, in linen, on foot, looking at everything with no eyes at the pace the heat allows. Do not wave. In summer you may nod, once, if the nod is also slow. The patrol appreciates being understood at the correct speed.","46": "The Solstice Picnic is held on the full moon, so the year's longest day is followed by its brightest night and the picnic simply continues through both. The pamphleteers of Sollen's Mouth move their monthly meeting onto the grass, where they agree on nothing outdoors, with sandwiches. Bring a blanket, a lantern for later, and one opinion you are prepared to lose beautifully.","47": "The day after the Picnic is the longest light of the year and the shortest shadows, and citizens are advised to file both while they can be told apart. Debts come due at noon, when no one can hide in anyone else's shade. The collectors wear white. It is the month's most honest day and its least comfortable, and the Bureau schedules it directly after the party on purpose.","48": "The escorted Deep City visit is the month's most contested ticket: one hour, underground, at cellar temperature. The escort does not answer questions and in summer does not need to; citizens return cool, quiet, and strangely rested, and the Howling Rites that evening are noticeably gentler.","50": "The Music Festival opens and runs a full week, the 22nd through the 28th, and the whole city is tuned for it: doors, gates, queues, and at least one Bureau staircase are brought to pitch by licensed tuners. Citizens are reminded that a tuned queue is still a queue. It merely waits in harmony.","51": "Festival, day two, and the streets keep time: the tuners walk their rounds at dawn and the cartwheels, gates, and Bureau staircase are brought back to the district key before the first sets. Citizens whose own instruments wander overnight may present them at the corner stands. Wandering is not fined during Festival week. It is corrected, kindly, in public.","52": "On the Festival's Sunsday the Pseudo-Religious Service is held on the green in the middle of the music, and for one morning the city's worship and the city's festival are the same event wearing each other's hats. The Temple of the Suns sends its regards and, traditionally, its best singers, who are not required to win anything.","53": "The Selenarian Choir's Gala crowns the Festival week: an evening of song in the high register, sung precisely, at prices that are also precise. The quarter moon attends as a balcony light. Citizens dress formally and weep at the grammar, and the Choir — who sing the language the way other choirs sing weather — accept the weeping as a review.","56": "The Festival closes, the tournament holds its final under paper lanterns, and the In-Transit House auctions the month's leavings: unclaimed picnic blankets, one tuned staircase (buyer collects), and three opinions, gently used. Bid in Teeth, in feathers, in Twists. Bid like summer is ending, because by the calendar's count it is filing itself away already.","57": "The filing season opens on the 1st of Zipsdier and the month opens three times, in three filings of twenty-eight days each, because one opening was found, by long experience, to be insufficient warning. Rent is due. The first Deep City signup window opens. Stamp something. The season has begun and it keeps its own appointments.","59": "The season's first Pseudo-Religious Service is a filing service: hymns are submitted in duplicate and sung from the stamped copy. The congregation's voices are recorded, which in this city is the same thing as being heard twice, once now and once forever.","60": "The white moon is new over the First Filing. Begin the season in the dark like everyone else: by touch, by queue, by memory of where the windows are. The dark is refiled three times this month. You will get better at it.","62": "The Howling Rites open the season as they open everything: in chorus, outdoors, at volume. In Zipsdier the Rites fall six times, once for each filing's rising, and the choirmaster calls this arrangement the season's spine. The neighbors call it other things, in harmony.","63": "The Factions Tournament runs every Ghoulday of the season, twelve tables across three filings, and the standings are kept on a board too large for any one room, which is displayed in three rooms and argued about in all of them.","65": "The Aria Distribution Allowance is paid three times in Zipsdier, once per filing. Bring two forms each time: six forms across the season, a complete set, which the clerks accept as a kind of devotion. Keep your receipts. The season is long and memory is a filing system with opinions.","67": "Quarter moon over the First Filing. A quarter of the filing is filed, which the Bureau celebrates by stamping the quarter itself. Citizens are reminded that a quarter filed is not a quarter excused; the remaining three quarters know where you live.","69": "The Elder Raven hears confessions three times in Zipsdier, once per filing, and regulars are advised that the season's middle confession is historically the honest one. It does not absolve. It files. By the Third Filing, your file and its file agree about you, which citizens describe as a relief of an unusual shape.","70": "Tournament, and stamps are provided at the tables — the season's courtesy, withdrawn in the Second Filing and mourned in the Third. Play proceeds at filing pace: every winning hand is recorded before it is celebrated, which has improved the celebrations and ruined several bluffs.","74": "The moon is full over the First Filing and the pamphleteers of Sollen's Mouth hold their meeting by its light, filing their minutes in triplicate because the season demands it. They agree on nothing. The minutes are nevertheless identical, which the pamphleteers cite as their whole theology.","76": "The escorted visit to the Deep City departs at the hour on your ticket and returns one hour later. In filing season the tickets are stamped at departure and again at return, and the two stamps never quite match, which the Bureau calls proof of travel. The escort does not answer questions. File them. Everything else down there is.","77": "Tournament day, and card copies are accepted at the tables for the only time in the season: a filed copy of a card plays exactly as the original, provided the copy is stamped and the player believes in it at regulation strength. Appeals are heard at the door, by the door.","81": "Quarter moon, and the First Filing leans toward its close. The Bureau reminds citizens that what is filed in the first twenty-eight days may be corrected in the second, at correction prices, and mourned in the third, free of charge.","84": "The First Filing closes at sundown. The In-Transit House folds its monthly auction into filing business, and the First Filing's unclaimed things — unsigned intentions, one complete duplicate citizen-file, a staircase (buyer collects) — go under the hammer between the closing stamps.","85": "The Second Filing opens. The Deep City signup window reopens for three days, because the season believes in second chances and files them in the middle. New ledgers are issued. The old ledgers are not collected. A citizen of Zipsdier carries both, and walks accordingly.","86": "The Nawn Coalition patrols the Second Filing on new routes, filed the night before by clerks who have never walked them, on foot, in silence, looking at everything with no eyes. Do not wave. If a patrol route crosses your street twice in one night, the Bureau's position is that your street was interesting. Accept the compliment and draw the curtains.","88": "The white moon is new again, the season's second dark, and citizens who began the First Filing by touch now navigate it by habit. The Bureau notes the improvement and files it under training, which is how the Bureau says grace.","91": "The Junior High Lock-in & Retreat is held tonight, with the tournament running concurrently as is traditional. The Retreat guarantees that 17 of every 19 children will be returned, and 14 of them safely. The guarantee is read aloud at the door, in full, by a clerk who does not pause at the arithmetic. The other parents have been notified.","93": "The Aria Distribution Allowance, second payment. Bring two forms; keep the third filing's copies clean. The queue at Window 2 in the Second Filing is historically the season's most philosophical, and citizens are asked not to resolve anything permanent while standing in it.","95": "Quarter moon, and half the season is filed: the exact midpoint of the longest month, marked by a stamp so heavy it is applied by two clerks and a lever. Citizens may watch. Watching is also filed.","97": "The Elder Raven's second Confessional Hours of the season. The middle confession, the honest one. Bring the version of events you have been carrying since the First Filing and set it down. The Raven will file it where it belongs, which is rarely where you were carrying it.","98": "Quarter Day, the season's hinge: the quarter-turn of the city's count falls inside Zipsdier's Second Filing this year, and the tournament plays at the bell. Balances are struck, rates are turned, and the Committee reminds citizens that a quarter is a direction as much as a fraction.","102": "Reversed Inspection Day falls on the Second Filing's full moon, and the order of inspection is reversed: citizens inspect the Bureau. Windows are examined, drawers are opened by the public, the pamphleteers file their minutes from the other side of the counter, beautifully, and every finding is stamped by the inspected, which is the Bureau's one day of being filed. The moon, full, supervises both directions equally.","104": "The escorted Deep City visit and the Howling Rites share the day, as they shared the season's first: descent by ticket in the afternoon, chorus by moonlight in the evening. Citizens returning from below howl with a notable steadiness. The choirmaster has stopped asking why.","105": "The midseason table is the tournament's sternest appointment: only citizens whose forms are complete — all six, both filings — may sit. Play is excellent and appeals are rare, because everyone at the table has read everyone else's paperwork, and plays accordingly.","107": "Other Skin Day in the Second Filing: skins are issued stamped this month, the season's one concession to traceability, and citizens spend the day as someone else under official seal. Return it by sundown, stamped, with the form. The stamp is the skin's own signature. Do not forge it, even kindly.","110": "The Lost-and-Found Lottery. Once each season, the In-Transit House's unclaimed holdings that survived both filings' auctions are drawn by lot: describe what you have lost, in writing, and the House draws. Citizens win back coats, names, one (1) childhood address. What is not claimed by description was, the House rules, never truly lost. It was in transit. It is always in transit.","113": "The Third Filing opens, the last of the season, and the final Deep City signup window opens with it, for three days only. After this window the year's remaining descents are by appointment with nobody. Sign up, or be content with the surface, which is also filed.","115": "The Deep City signup window closes tonight, at the close of the service, and the last names of the season go onto the list. Citizens walking home afterward sometimes speak of a bus seen on the road out of the district, unlit, going somewhere the timetable does not keep. The Bureau files such reports under weather. Tomorrow the Linesong begins, and the city turns its face to older passengers than any bus.","116": "Linesong, the first night, falls on the white moon's third new moon of the season: the darkest appointment of the year, kept deliberately. The city sings the names of its dead home, low, from the stamped copies, and the new moon is the correct light for it, which is to say none, which is to say the singing is the light.","117": "Linesong, the second night. The names are sung home, the doors are left as the songs require, and the city is gentle with itself in a way it practices all year to afford for two nights. What citizens hear after the last name is their own business and is filed nowhere. The Bureau insists on this. It is the season's one privacy, and the Bureau guards it like a window.","119": "Tournament in the Third Filing, and echoes are permitted at the tables for the only week of the season: a play may be answered by the room. The Linesong is two days past and the city's voice is still doubled. The standings, the clerks note, have never been more tenderly disputed.","121": "The Aria Distribution Allowance, final payment of the season. Bring two forms; the set is complete. Citizens who have collected all three payments hold, in aggregate, the season's blessing in triplicate, and Window 2 stamps the aggregate once, gently, like closing a book.","123": "Quarter moon over the Third Filing: the last quarter of the longest month. The Old Ones are not in this season's story at all, which the clerks note without relief. Some absences are also appointments. File accordingly.","125": "The Elder Raven's final Confessional Hours of the season. The third confession is historically the quiet one: citizens set down what the year has made of them and the Raven files it in the year's own hand. It does not absolve. By now, nobody is asking it to. The filing is the absolution, and the season has taught everyone to hear it.","130": "The moon is full for the last time in Zipsdier, and the pamphleteers of Sollen's Mouth close their season's book at their meeting: three filings of minutes, agreed upon in no particular, bound in one volume. It is available at the door. It is the season's truest history and it contradicts itself on every page, in writing, beautifully.","132": "The season's last escorted Deep City visit and its last Howling Rites share the day, the descent in the afternoon and the chorus after dark, and citizens who do both are stamped twice and howl once, which the choirmaster accepts as correct proportion.","135": "The Council is in the building. This is known the way weather is known: the queues shorten themselves, the clerks' handwriting improves, and no one files anything frivolous for two days. The Closed Session follows on the 81st. The building does not announce the Council. The building behaves, and the behavior is the announcement.","137": "The Council's Closed Session falls on the Third Filing's quarter moon. The Council of 9 sits; the doors are shut; the quarter moon keeps the corridor. What the Council files in closed session is filed. Citizens are reminded that the session is not a secret; it is a closure, which is a different instrument entirely, and older.","140": "The Zipsdier Close. The season ends, the Third Filing shuts, and the Annual Return is due today, complete, in person, at the window that has been expecting you since the 1st. The last tournament table plays beside the queue. File everything. What is unfiled at the Close is carried into the next month by hand, by clerks, at carrying prices, and everyone in the city can hear the carrying.","141": "Co-Signing Day. Every filing of the new season opens with two signatures: yours, and one citizen who agrees, in writing, to be sorry alongside you if it goes wrong. Rent is also due, and the Deep City signup window opens, because Mourndier believes a month should begin with all its obligations holding hands.","142": "If you signed for someone yesterday, today is for checking what you signed. The Bureau permits one careful look. The Bureau recommends it.","144": "On the new moon the mortality rolls are read aloud at the district office: every citizen filed as deceased in the season past, in alphabetical order, at a respectful pace. The Council grieves and invoices in the same sitting. Attendance is not required. Absence is noted kindly, and then it is noted.","145": "The reading continues by district until every name has had its turn. Citizens are encouraged to check the spelling of their own entry in advance. Corrections filed by the deceased are accepted without comment. Corrections filed by the living are accepted with one.","146": "The Howling Rites this month are held in memoriam, outdoors, at the usual pitch. Citizens who did not know the deceased are welcome. In Mourndier, that is most of the howling, and it is considered the finest kind.","147": "The weekly tournament opens the season. Winners co-sign the losers' condolence forms, which is the tournament's oldest rule and the reason everyone plays carefully.","148": "Coupon day at the cemetery. The groundskeepers accept the Committee's coupons for plot-side services, wreath adjustments, and one guided walk among the stones. The dead do not redeem coupons. The dead are, however, present for the savings, and the Bureau counts this as participation.","149": "The Aria Distribution Allowance is paid at Window 2. In Mourndier, bring a witness as well as two forms: grief makes clerks generous, and generosity must be countersigned.","150": "A Repetition Day. By order of the unicorns, today is Hendiber the 3rd again: the Thanking of the Stairs, and a Sunsday besides. Citizens will thank the stairs they use, attend the service they attended, and file what they filed. The Bureau's whole guidance is the official blessing: good luck.","152": "The Nawn Coalition patrols Mourndier in silence, reading the rolls over shoulders as they pass. Walk slower than usual. A nawn who stops beside you is checking a spelling, and the Bureau asks you to hold still for it the way you would for a tailor.","153": "The Elder Raven hears confessions on the 13th. In Mourndier the queue is long and mostly quiet. The Raven files what it hears under M, for mortality, regardless of content. Citizens sleep better anyway.","155": "The Bazaar of Second Prices. Everything at the bazaar has been priced once, found wanting, and priced again — lower, usually, and more honestly. Haggle twice: once for the object, once for its history. The second haggle is the bazaar.","156": "Debts carried out of Gloomber may be re-filed today at the kinder rate. The Bureau does not call this mercy. The Bureau calls it the sixteenth.","157": "Don't Sleep Day. Just don't. The Bureau offers no substitute activity and no explanation, and the one clerk who drafted one was reassigned to the rolls. Citizens spend the day awake together, in shifts of attention, being tired at one another with great courtesy. By sundown nobody remembers deciding anything, which the Bureau files as the point.","158": "The moon is full and the pamphleteers of Sollen's Mouth hold their monthly meeting, at which they read the rolls and agree on nothing about them, in writing, beautifully. Their annotated edition of the rolls is the month's favorite literature and its least reliable.","160": "The escorted visit to the Deep City departs at the hour on your ticket and returns one hour later. In Mourndier the escort carries a lantern and a list of the rolls, out of courtesy, in case anyone down there is on it. The Howling Rites follow at dusk for those who came back thoughtful.","161": "The weekly tournament is played in Mourndier rules this week: a lost game is mourned for one full minute, by both players, standing. Then tea. Then the next game.","162": "Other Skin Day. You will be issued a skin at the district office, sized by estimate, and in Mourndier citizens are asked to choose the skin of someone on the rolls if one remains unclaimed. You will wear their errands for a day. Return it by sundown, dry, with the form. What you learn in it is yours to keep and never mention.","164": "The Pseudo-Religious Service is held with candles this week, one per name read on the 4th that anyone present can remember. The candles are not the doctrine. The remembering is not the doctrine either. The doctrine is filed separately and the service goes on without it, warmly.","166": "The Nawn Coalition patrols again, slower now, near the month's end. Stand still when they pass. A citizen who stands still enough may be filed, briefly, as architecture, which in Mourndier is a compliment the dead also receive.","168": "The weekly tournament closes the month, and the In-Transit House auction follows in the same hall: unclaimed effects, forwarded luggage, and one sealed parcel from the Deep City visit that nobody will bid on until somebody does. Proceeds are co-signed. Everything in Mourndier is co-signed. That is the month working as intended.","169": "Rent is due, the Deep City signup window opens, and Gloomber opens with them: the deep gloom, the city's annual low season of the heart. Citizens are advised to begin the month the way the Bureau does — seated, lamp lit, expectations filed at the lower rate.","170": "Screaming is favorable today and, in Gloomber, barely requires the approved receptacle; the gloom absorbs what the fog elsewhere returns to the wrong address. Citizens scream once, well, and then get on with the month. The Bureau considers this the gloom's one civic efficiency.","171": "The Agencies hold their annual debate on a Sunsday, in the service hall, the service having been respectfully rescheduled to the corridor. The debate is refereed. Both sides lose. The referees file the result under G, for gloom, and the corridor service is, by all accounts, the year's best attended, because everyone is already there and nobody is allowed to leave cheerful.","172": "When the white moon is new in Gloomber, the dark is doubled: the month's own gloom over the moon's absence. The Bureau lights the district lamps at noon. Citizens walk as if underwater, politely, and collisions are forgiven in advance.","173": "The Nawn Coalition patrols the gloom on foot, in silence, which in Gloomber means they are visible chiefly as a slight improvement in the darkness. Do not wave. They are working. If the dark beside you straightens up, that was a patrol.","174": "The Howling Rites are held this month for morale, outdoors, at the usual pitch, with lanterns. The choirmaster describes the turnout as brave. The neighbors describe it as audible. Both filings stand.","176": "Once a year the city's gloom is appraised: its depth, its texture, its resale value as atmosphere. Citizens are asked to hold still and be gloomy in a representative fashion. The appraisal sets the lamp tax, the soup price, and the tone of the month's mandate reports.","177": "The Aria Distribution Allowance is paid at Window 2. Bring two forms. In Gloomber the queue moves at the speed of the season and nobody minds, which the Bureau files as the gloom paying its own dividend.","178": "The Pseudo-Religious Service is held indoors, dimly, attendance taken by candlelight and therefore approximately. The doctrine is read at half volume. Citizens report it is the service they feel most, which the service files without surprise.","179": "Other Skin Day falls on the quarter moon, and you will be issued a skin at the district office, sized by estimate — in Gloomber, skins run gloomier than their owners, and citizens are advised to allow for this at the mirror. Return it by sundown, dry, with the form. What you learn in it is yours to keep and never mention.","181": "Moonsday the 13th. The Elder Raven hears confessions today, and the calendar's oldest argument keeps its seat beside it: the filing insists the 13th of Gloomber is a Moonsday, the moon's own testimony disagrees, and the matter is filed DISPUTED and has never been settled. Citizens observe the day under whichever weekday they can defend. The Raven accepts confessions under both.","182": "The weekly tournament is played by lantern throughout the month; this week, the losers dim first, as a courtesy, so the winners may see their victory clearly. The rule is old. Nobody remembers voting for it. Nobody would repeal it.","184": "The Nawn Coalition patrols again, unseen, mostly — a citizen may spend the whole patrol as a coat rack in the dark and be thanked for it in writing, eventually. The Bureau confirms these thank-yous are sincere. The Bureau confirms everything in Gloomber eventually. It is that kind of month.","186": "The moon is full — the one bright night of the deep gloom — and the Gloom-Off is held in its light: a contest of sustained gloom, refereed, silent, won by the citizen who holds the longest face against the full moon's best efforts. The reigning champion has not smiled since the incident. The pamphleteers of Sollen's Mouth attend in lieu of their monthly meeting and take notes in the dark, from memory, wrongly, beautifully.","188": "The escorted visit to the Deep City departs at the hour on your ticket and returns one hour later. Gloomber citizens are considered seasoned for the trip and are issued no lantern; the Deep City, the escort notes, is darker, and it is a point of local pride that nobody from this month has ever asked for the hour to be shortened. The Howling Rites follow at dusk, at half pitch, out of respect for the hour's survivors.","189": "The Junior High Lock-in & Retreat is held tonight, and the weekly tournament runs concurrently in the same building, by the district's ancient double-booking, which no office will admit to signing. The Retreat guarantees that 17 of every 19 children will be returned, and 14 of them safely. The tournament guarantees a winner. Citizens are asked to count heads at both doors and to keep the counts in separate columns.","190": "The Headmaster's Day Off. The Bureau operates at half voice. Filings are accepted gently, stamps descend slowly, and any citizen may be excused one (1) sigh at any window, no form required. Where the Headmaster goes on this day is not filed. The clerks' account — that the day is spent being filed, somewhere, as a citizen — is printed here as an account, and the Committee rules on it no more than the clerks do.","191": "Second Helpings Day. Every citizen is entitled to a second helping of the month: one more serving of soup, of candlelight, of whatever the season has been serving, on presentation of the form. Seconds of the gloom itself are available at Window 2 and are, the Bureau notes with something like pride, the least requested item on any menu in the city.","193": "The quarter moon returns and the gloom begins, officially, to thin — though in Gloomber \"thin\" is a technical term meaning a citizen may now distinguish the patrol from the weather at ten paces. Lamps are still lit at noon. Morale is filed as improving, in pencil.","195": "The collectors make their rounds at gloom rates: settlements gentler, smiles rarer, arithmetic kinder. Settle what you owe while the season is soft. The collectors' courtesy in Gloomber is famous and is not, the Bureau wishes it noted, a trap.","196": "Quarter Day. The month closes its quarter at the windows: filings due, accounts squared, the quarter's gloom weighed against the quarter's candles. The weekly tournament plays its final in the square and the In-Transit House auction follows in the same hall, where the unclaimed lots this quarter include three lanterns, one umbrella of unusual size, and a coat that several citizens insist is standing in it.","197": "The Steep opens the tea season, and rent is due, and the Deep City signup window opens, all on the same morning, because Teadber is a sociable month and likes its queues with company. The first pot of the season is poured at the district office at nine. Citizens who miss it are not behind. The season is long and the pot is refilled.","198": "The Steep's rule is old: one invitation, one teapot, no plus-ones. The invitation admits the citizen; the teapot admits their mood. Guests who bring a second teapot are seated at the second table, which is widely considered the better one and is never, ever described that way by the hosts.","199": "The Pseudo-Religious Service is followed by tea, poured by the ushers, strength at the service's discretion. The doctrine is read first. The tea is the doctrine's longest-standing commentary and the only one citizens are permitted to take home.","200": "On the new moon the first leaves of the season are read at the district office: a small cup, a dark sky, a clerk with clean hands. What the leaves say on the 4th sets the season's opening price and is binding on no one, which the Bureau calls the correct relationship between a citizen and a forecast.","201": "In Teadber the kettle files its intentions early: households register the day's first boiling by leaving the whistle unsilenced until the parrots have counted it. A silent kettle is not an offense. It is merely, for one month of the year, a rumor.","202": "The Howling Rites are held outdoors, at the usual pitch, with tea served at the interval in the good cups. Citizens howl, drink, and howl again, and the choirmaster's filed opinion — that tea improves the second howl measurably — has never been contradicted by anyone who stayed for both.","203": "The weekly tournament opens the season at the long table, teapots at either end, the pot itself an unofficial trophy that returns to the Bureau each week having seen things. Players are reminded that a spilled cup is a forfeited move and a stained form is a confession.","205": "The Aria Distribution Allowance is paid at Window 2. Bring two forms. In Teadber the queue is served tea by the window clerks, in rotation, and a citizen's place may be held by their cup. A cup found unattended holds the place of somebody loved.","207": "The quarter moon is observed with half a cup, precisely: the district offices measure, and citizens who pour generously are corrected with great tenderness and a smaller cup. The moon, the Bureau notes, is also at half, and managing beautifully.","208": "The Nawn Coalition patrols between teas, on the hour, in silence, carrying nothing. Do not wave. A nawn at your table's end is checking the leaves, and it is considered poor form to ask what it sees. It sees the same leaves. It reads them slower.","209": "The Elder Raven hears confessions on the 13th. In Teadber the Raven takes tea first, black, and citizens report that confession pours easier after. The Raven files what it hears and wipes the cup's rim with its own cloth, which it brought, and which it will not discuss.","210": "Father's Day. The city honors fathers as filed: by blood, by countersignature, by having once stood at a stove or a window and been pointed at by a child and named correctly. Gifts are given at the midday meal, thanks are settled — Ulvor, always Ulvor — and the weekly tournament plays after supper, at the family table, with the father awarded the first move and the last word, both of which he is expected to spend kindly.","211": "The leaves are read again at mid-season and decline to elaborate on the 4th. The Bureau accepts this. A forecast that repeats itself is a rumor; a forecast that goes quiet is a professional.","212": "A longer steep is favorable today: leaves left standing past the usual count, cups poured dark, conversations allowed to finish their sentences. The Bureau recommends the day to citizens with something unfinished that is not a form.","214": "The moon is full and the pamphleteers of Sollen's Mouth hold their monthly meeting over tea, at which they agree on nothing and refill everything. Their minutes this month are kept on the pot's tally card and are the season's favorite literature: disputed, annotated, and stained in three places that the pamphleteers describe as emphasis.","215": "Other Skin Day. You will be issued a skin at the district office, sized by estimate, and in Teadber one rule is added to the usual ones: return it unstained. Tea in another's skin is taken standing, at a counter, over a saucer, with respect for the previous tenant's laundry. Return it by sundown, dry, with the form. What you learn in it is yours to keep and never mention.","216": "The escorted visit to the Deep City departs at the hour on your ticket and returns one hour later. Teadber citizens are served nothing down there and ask for nothing, having been raised by a month that teaches the difference between a visit and a residence. The Howling Rites follow at dusk, and the first cup back is poured for whoever looks coldest, by acclamation.","217": "The weekly tournament is played for the pot: the season's traveling teapot, engraved with the names of tables it has outlasted. The winner holds it for one week and files a report on its behavior. The reports are collected, bound, and considered by the pamphleteers to be the finest prose in the city, which the pamphleteers would say.","219": "The Nawn Coalition patrols again. Do not offer tea. The nawns do not drink, being otherwise employed by the act of looking, and an offered cup places a citizen in the delicate position of holding it forever, out of politeness, until the patrol has passed the district entirely.","220": "The Pseudo-Religious Service is held with the good cups, brought down from the office shelf for the season's last stretch. Breakage at the service is forgiven in advance and recorded in pencil. The service holds that a good cup, like a good filing, is meant to be used at the edge of its strength.","222": "Borrowed cups are returned by sundown today, washed, with the borrower's name filed inside the saucer. A cup returned late is a story. A cup returned early is a compliment. A cup not returned is, by Teadber law older than the Bureau, a relative.","223": "The last leaves of the season are read and offer no comment, and the Bureau files the silence as the season's most accurate forecast. Prices are settled on it anyway. Citizens nod at their cups the way clerks nod at a stamp: something has been decided by someone qualified.","224": "As-Filed Night. The season closes exactly as it stands: no improvement, no garnish, no second drafting of the month. The weekly tournament plays its final in the square, the In-Transit House holds its auction after — unclaimed cups, forwarded kettles, one parcel that ticks faintly and is sold, as always, to the Bureau — and at midnight the whole month is taken as filed, which in Teadber is the highest compliment the city pays anything: it was enough, as it was.","225": "Rent is due, the Deep City signup window opens, and the Recopying begins, all on one morning, because the Bureau believes a citizen with a pen in hand is a citizen accounted for. Sign early. Copy carefully. The year you save may be your own file.","226": "On the second of Quillder every ledger in the city is opened and every page judged against its copy. Pages that disagree with themselves are not punished. They are recopied, which the pages describe as the same thing, in a kinder hand.","227": "Other Skin Day falls on a Sunsday this year. You will attend the Pseudo-Religious Service in the skin the district office issued you, sized by estimate, and the sermon will be addressed to whoever you appear to be. The Bureau counts this as outreach.","228": "When the white moon is new, the copyists work by touch, in the dark, and their copies are considered the truest of the month. A copy you cannot see cannot flatter you.","229": "Being Your Soul to Work Day. Citizens bring their souls to work, in the container of their choice, and file them at the desk before beginning. Souls are inspected at noon, returned at the close of business slightly used, and initialed. Absent souls must be explained in writing, by the body, which is considered poor form.","230": "The Howling Rites are held outdoors. In Quillder, copies of the howls are provided at the gate for citizens who have forgotten theirs. The originals are kept by the choir, who are not generous.","232": "computah skillz day. The name arrived at the Bureau spelled exactly so, on a form no clerk remembers accepting, concerning a device no office admits to owning. The day is observed by citizens who possess computahs, in ways the Committee cannot describe and has stopped investigating. The name is printed as received. The Bureau files what it does not understand with the same care as what it does. More, on bad years.","233": "The Aria Distribution Allowance is paid at Window 2. Bring two forms: one for the Aria, one certifying that this year's copy of you is the current edition.","235": "At the quarter moon, half of every ledger in the city stands recopied. The Bureau celebrates quietly, in duplicate.","236": "The Nawn Coalition patrols Quillder on foot, in silence. Copyists are reminded that when a nawn passes the window, pens are set down. Not from fear. From respect for a colleague who files without hands.","237": "The Elder Raven hears confessions on the 13th, and the 13th of Quillder is filed DISPUTED: some filings hold it is a Moonsday, others hold the dispute itself is the filing, and the Bureau prints the dispute rather than the answer. The Raven hears confessions either way. The Raven has seniority over the calendar.","239": "Once a year the Index requires every citizen to copy their own file, by hand, in full, with no witnesses. Errors you make copying yourself are legally yours twice. Most citizens report the exercise restful. Most citizens are copying that report from last year's.","240": "A Repetition Day. By order of the unicorns, today is Sunmanber the 18th again: the Solstice Picnic, repeated in the copying month, out of season and entirely official. Citizens picnic among the ledgers, on blankets, in their copying clothes. The picnic is filed twice. The ants are filed once and have appealed. Good luck.","242": "The moon is full and the pamphleteers of Sollen's Mouth hold their monthly meeting, at which the year's minutes are recopied from memory, argued over, and recopied again correctly, which is to say differently. Their final text is beautiful and binding on no one.","243": "Fresh ink is favorable today and old errors are at their most legible. A good day to read your own file the way an enemy would, and forgive the penmanship.","244": "The escorted visit to the Deep City departs at the hour on your ticket and returns one hour later. The Howling Rites are held afterward, outdoors, and citizens returning from below are excused from copying their howls. Some howl from memory. It is not the same, and everyone pretends it is.","247": "Errors discovered in any ledger today, if confessed at the window before sundown, are forgiven once. The forgiveness is recorded in the margin, in the forgiving clerk's best hand, where it will shame the error forever.","250": "Signatures fade in Quillder like everything else. Check yours twice today: once for the hand, once for the intent. The Bureau recognizes a signature by its hesitation and forgives both kinds.","251": "Leave Your Toddler at the Canyon Weekend begins. Drop-off is at the Canyon Gate from dawn, where toddlers are received by the Canyon Wardens, labeled kindly, and issued a blanket and a number. The canyon is shaded, staffed, and experienced. Parents receive a receipt and the Committee's warmest wishes.","252": "The Weekend concludes: toddlers are returned at the closing bell, counted twice, in keeping with the month. The In-Transit House auction follows at dusk, where unclaimed copies of things are sold to citizens who resemble their descriptions.","253": "Rent is due, and the Deep City signup window opens into Frostder's first cold. The list fills with citizens who find the deep warm by comparison. Sign early. The ice on the steps is filed as a crowd-control measure, and the Bureau leaves it there on purpose.","254": "In Frostder the frost itself keeps the windows, inscribing each pane overnight in a hand no clerk admits to learning. The inscriptions are read at breakfast, argued over, and melted by noon. Nothing they have ever said has been wrong. Nothing they have ever said has been kept.","256": "When the white moon is new in Frostder, the dark arrives early and stays late, like a relative. Lamps are lit at windows all over the city, less for the citizens than for the dark, so it can find its way around without taking anything.","258": "The Howling Rites are held outdoors in all weathers. In Frostder the breath of the choir is visible, and the howls can be watched leaving, which the choirmaster considers a teaching aid and the neighbors consider a forecast.","260": "The Day of Other Places. Once a year the city practices being somewhere else: doors are labeled with the names of elsewhere, windows are dressed as other weather, and citizens address each other by the customs of places none of them has been. Letters may be posted today to places you are not, and are answered, eventually, in unfamiliar hands.","261": "The Aria Distribution Allowance is paid at Window 2. Bring two forms, gloved: the cold takes fingerprints first and paperwork second, and the Bureau wishes to receive both in working order.","263": "At the quarter moon, half the river is ice and half is argument. Citizens cross on the ice where the ravens have marked it, and argue about the rest from the banks, warmly.","264": "The Nawn Coalition patrols Frostder on foot, in silence, and the snow avoids them: their route stays bare all winter, a dark line through the white districts, which the Bureau maps and the plows respect. Do not wave. They are working.","265": "The Elder Raven hears confessions on the 13th, by firelight, in the season when citizens have the most to confess and the least wish to say it outdoors. The Raven does not absolve. It files, close to the fire, where the confessions stay warm.","267": "Soup is favorable today and second thoughts are at their most nourishing. The Bureau recommends both in the same hour, at the same table, in the company of whoever wronged you mildly.","270": "The Festival of Starlight falls on the longest night of the year, under a full moon, and the city meets it with every light it owns: lamps, candles, lanterns, and the white moon doing the work of all of them. The pamphleteers of Sollen's Mouth hold their monthly meeting by starlight and agree on nothing, beautifully, in the cold. The Temple of the Suns keeps its lamps burning all night in honor of the absent suns, who are expected back by morning and are.","271": "The mandatory silence around the aged tridium begins. It is observed around it, at a respectful distance. Citizens speak in filings for three days: notes passed hand to hand, petitions written small, apologies left where they will be found. The city does not get quieter. It gets more legible.","272": "The silence continues. The escorted visit to the Deep City departs on time, in silence, and returns one hour later the same way, which returning citizens describe as the quietest quiet of the year. The Community Howling Rites are observed tonight in writing, in full, and filed with the choir.","273": "The silence lifts at sundown, and the Factions Tournament is held in the evening for citizens rationing their first words carefully. Most spend them on the game. The Bureau considers this a healthy civic instinct and has never once said so out loud, until now, in print, which does not count.","275": "Do not feed the statues today. The Bureau does not elaborate. In Frostder the statues are at their most receptive, which is the entire reason, and also classified.","276": "Other Skin Day falls on a Sunsday this year. The district office issues skins with the season in mind: the borrowed are, on the whole, warmer. You will attend the Pseudo-Religious Service as somebody else, somebody better dressed against the cold, and the sermon will find you anyway. Return the skin by sundown, dry, with the form.","277": "At the quarter moon the cold keeps its counsel and the city keeps its lamps lit a little longer against it. Debts of warmth are remembered kindly in Frostder and called in slowly.","278": "The collectors make house calls in Frostder wearing scarves, and settling indoors is considered polite on all sides. A good day to owe less by evening than you owed at breakfast.","280": "The In-Transit House auction is held by lamplight, and the tournament finals follow, in gloves, before a fire the auction house provides and itemizes. Lots that glow are sold as-is, which in Frostder is most of them.","281": "The Day of Being Right is also rent day and the opening of the Deep City signup window, and the Bureau would not have it any other way: every citizen begins Shroudber correct, housed or pursued, and first in line for the deep. See the Special Days. Bring your one right thing and your rent; both are received at the same window, with the same expression.","282": "The fog in Shroudber signs nothing — fog cannot hold a pen and has never claimed otherwise — but it witnesses everything, and its testimony is accepted at every window in the city. Citizens conduct themselves accordingly, which is to say better than their leases require.","284": "When the white moon is new, the fog files the sky in its place and the city proceeds on the fog's word. Appointments made under a filed sky are binding. Apologies for the sky are not accepted; the fog keeps its own counsel and its own hours.","286": "The Howling Rites are held outdoors, leases permitting: several districts hold their rites on land whose agreements specify volume, hours, and the treatment of echoes. The echoes are tenants in good standing and are howled back to politely.","287": "The Junior High Lock-in & Retreat is held tonight, in a hall the Bureau selects annually and describes annually as adequate. The Retreat guarantees that 17 of every 19 children will be returned, and 14 of them safely. The other parents have been notified. The Factions Tournament runs concurrently in the same building, separated by a curtain, a rulebook, and mutual respect.","288": "The Nawn Coalition patrols Shroudber's fog on foot, in silence, distinguishable from the fog mainly by intention. Citizens are reminded that a shape in the fog that does not move toward you is working. A shape that does is also working. Walk on.","289": "Other Skin Day falls on the Aria Distribution day this month, and Window 2 serves both queues, which alternate: a skin, an allowance, a skin, an allowance, with the patience the Bureau is famous for. You will draw your Aria as somebody else this month, and the forms account for it; the Bureau thought of everything except a shorter line.","292": "Every lease in the city carries the fog clause, concerning what the fog may enter, witness, and keep. Find yours today, while the month is young and the fog is still only reading over your shoulder.","293": "The Elder Raven hears confessions on the 13th, and in Shroudber the confessions are received sealed: spoken into the fog, receipted by the Raven, and filed where the fog keeps its own copies. Citizens report sleeping better anyway.","294": "Quarter Day: the quarter's accounts are squared at the registry, leases are measured against the rooms they describe, and discrepancies are introduced to each other formally. The Factions Tournament is held at the registry afterward, so that citizens may lose at something smaller before sundown.","296": "Letters from the Oscu estate are expected this month, as they are every Shroudber, arriving the way they arrive: in the fog, by hand, unsigned for, and found signed-for already. Citizens receiving one are reminded that Lease Night is the 28th, that the letters keep until then, and that opening one early is permitted and unwise.","298": "The moon is full and the pamphleteers of Sollen's Mouth hold their monthly meeting, devoted this month to leases: they dispute every clause in the city, beautifully, in writing, and bind no one. Landlords attend in disguise. The disguises are also disputed.","299": "Do not sign in the fog today. Signatures witnessed only by fog are honored by the fog, in the fog's jurisdiction, under the fog's terms, which are extensive. The Bureau's clerks will be indoors, dry, and available. Use them.","300": "The escorted visit to the Deep City departs at the hour on your ticket and returns one hour later. The Howling Rites follow outdoors, and returning citizens howl with a certain authority this month, having recently been somewhere the fog is not.","303": "Forgiveness is provisionally available today at most windows, pending ratification, review, and the forgiveness of the forgiveness. Citizens report that provisional forgiveness feels exactly like the other kind until it doesn't, which the Bureau accepts as a definition.","305": "At the quarter moon the fog thins at the edges of the city, and citizens walk out to the thinning to look at the countryside the way one looks at a clause one is considering. It is still there. It is still binding.","307": "Provisional Approval Day: see the Special Days. Every application in the city is approved today, provisionally, which is the Bureau's favorite tense and Shroudber's whole philosophy in one stamp.","308": "Lease Night: see the Special Days. The In-Transit House auction adjourns early tonight, by tradition and by letter: some of its lots are leases, and the leases are otherwise engaged.","309": "The Forecast is published at dawn from the steps of the weather office: the month's calamities, sanctioned and scheduled in advance, so that citizens may plan around their disasters like civilized people. Rent is due the same morning, and the Deep City signup window opens. It is the busiest desk of the year.","310": "The Festival of Misaligned Consequences celebrates the city's oldest civic fact: consequences arrive, but rarely at the address on the envelope. The festival has run thirty years without an accident. No programme has ever been filed. The Committee maintains this is why it works.","311": "The Pseudo-Religious Service this month takes signs for its subject. Attendance is never predicted. It is merely expected, which the clergy find is the stronger instrument.","312": "The Forecast predicts nothing for today. The district reads this as the month's boldest prediction, and the shops close early out of respect for it.","313": "The Nawn Coalition patrols the routes where the Forecast has scheduled incidents. The incidents, out of courtesy, occur elsewhere. The Coalition files this as cooperation.","314": "The Howling Rites are held under a predicted-clear sky; bring a coat anyway. In Chandber the rites serve as the district's second weather instrument: if the choir is rained on, the Forecast buys the drinks.","315": "At the tournament, the odds are filed alongside the Forecast and are binding. A player who wins against the filed odds is congratulated warmly and quietly re-filed.","317": "The Aria Distribution Allowance is paid at Window 2. Bring two forms. In Chandber the amount is forecast in advance; any discrepancy between the forecast and the Aria may be appealed to the weather, which does not sit.","319": "Quarter moon. Half the month's calamities have now failed to occur on schedule. The Bureau calls this accuracy of a higher order and has promoted the clerk responsible.","320": "Today is not in the Forecast. Citizens are invited to enjoy it in an orderly fashion and to report any enjoyment at Window 3, for the record, where it will be added to next year's evidence.","321": "Whether today is Moonsday the 13th is disputed. The calendar says one thing; the moon's house says another; the Elder Raven hears confessions either way and files them under both dates. The Bureau rules on neither. Confess, and let the filing sort it out.","323": "Other Skin Day. Skins are issued at the district office, sized by estimate, with the day's forecast printed inside the collar. The forecast is never right either, which citizens find comforting in a borrowed skin. Return it by sundown, dry, with the form.","326": "The moon is full, and the pamphleteers of Sollen's Mouth meet to denounce the Forecast formally, in writing, beautifully. Their counter-forecast is published tomorrow. It is also wrong, and they would not have it any other way.","327": "Nothing is scheduled today. It is the month's blank page, and the old citizens call it the only honest day in Chandber. Spend it unrecorded, if you can.","328": "The Annual Anti-Shadoran Parade processes at noon, and the whole district turns out for it. The grudge is old, total, and kept in excellent repair. The escorted Deep City visit departs before the parade and returns after it; the escort's timing is not negotiable. The Howling Rites are held in the evening, in the parade's wake.","330": "The Forecast says fine. Citizens stay indoors. A fine forecast is the least trusted document in Chandber, and umbrellas are carried all month as a matter of doctrine, not weather.","333": "Quarter moon. The Forecast is reissued for the remainder of the month, with corrections. The corrections are also a forecast, and will be corrected in Feastber, which has its own concerns.","334": "The Nawn Coalition patrols again, and their report this month is a single page, mostly weather. They saw something coming. Nobody asks what. Asking is not the Coalition's custom, and answering is not either.","336": "The tournament closes the month, and the In-Transit House auction follows it. Lots this month include six umbrellas (unclaimed), one barometer that predicts only the past, and one (1) consequence, misaligned, still in its envelope.","337": "The Pricing is read aloud on the 1st: every dish, drink, and chair of the coming Confluence, priced and filed. Prices set at the Pricing are binding until the Confluence closes; haggling afterward is a different festival, held in another month. Rent is due the same morning, and the Deep City signup window opens. Feastber begins with three queues and an appetite.","339": "The Pseudo-Religious Service in Feastber is held at table whenever a table can be found, and in Feastber a table can always be found. Grace is sung before the meal and filed after it. The sermon is short. The choir is fed first.","340": "When the white moon is new, the kitchens go dark early, so the cooks may rest their eyes before the month brightens. A dark kitchen in Feastber is not a closure. It is a promise.","341": "The Nawn Coalition patrols the market rows while the Pricing disputes are settled around them. They taste nothing. Do not offer them a sample. They are working, and their refusal is a form of mercy.","342": "A Repetition Day. By order of the unicorns, today is Zipsdier the 1st again: filing season opens, briefly, inside the feast month, and everything filed today is filed in Zipsdier's hand. The Howling Rites are held in the evening regardless; a repeated day deserves a repeated noise. Good luck.","343": "At the tournament, the winners eat first: the week's priority at the long table is a filed prize, entered on the seating chart in ink. Losers are seated with honor, and slightly later, which in Feastber is a measurable distance.","345": "The Aria Distribution Allowance is paid at Window 2. Bring two forms. In Feastber the queue is catered. The caterers are not part of the allowance. They are part of the test.","347": "Quarter moon. Every oven in the district declares its capacity and its intentions before the Confluence opens, and the declarations are audited under a half-lit sky. Ovens that decline to declare are watched, warmly.","348": "The long tables are measured twice: once for length, and once for worthiness. Tables that fail the second measurement are used for the judges, as is traditional, and the judges pretend not to notice, as is also traditional.","349": "The Elder Raven hears confessions on the 13th, and in Feastber it hears them at the end of the long table, between courses. It does not absolve. It files, and passes the salt.","350": "The Annual Bake Sale is held at the Church of The Eversworn Beast: the year's great table before the great table. The parish ovens bake for a week beforehand. The tournament runs the same evening, and its players arrive powdered with flour, which is not a uniform. What the proceeds proceed to, the Church does not say. The Beast does not say either.","351": "The Cornic Confluence opens: fourteen days of sanctioned eating at the long tables. Seats are filed, courses are numbered, and strangers are seated together on purpose. The Committee reminds citizens that the Confluence is not a contest, except where it is, and those places are marked.","354": "The moon is full over the long table, and the pamphleteers of Sollen's Mouth hold their monthly meeting at it. This month their pamphlets are menus, printed beautifully, and they agree on nothing except dessert, on which they are unanimous and brief.","355": "The Loser's Bracket fills. Eaters defeated in the Confluence's contests are not dismissed; they are bracketed, and given their own table, their own honors, and — citizens insist, and the Committee does not correct them — the better conversation.","356": "The escorted visit to the Deep City departs at the hour on your ticket and returns one hour later. Eat first. The Deep City provides nothing, and the return appetite is famous in three districts. The Howling Rites follow in the evening, at table volume.","358": "By the Confluence's eighth day, leftovers achieve seniority. A dish served twice is addressed formally, may not be refused, and is seated above newer dishes regardless of merit. The Bureau calls this respect. The dishes call it nothing; they are busy.","361": "Quarter moon. The bracket narrows, and the field thins with the moon. What remains at the table now is seasoned, in every sense the Bureau recognizes and one it doesn't.","363": "Other Skin Day, in the middle of the Confluence. Skins are issued at the district office as usual, and today you will eat as someone else: their seat, their courses, their luck at the bracket. Return the skin by sundown, dry, with the form. Do not ask what you ate. You were delicious company.","364": "The Cornic Confluence closes at sundown, and the In-Transit House auction follows the final course. Lots this month include twelve chairs, one long table (measured, worthy), and the Bracket's spare crown. The last form of the feast is a napkin, filed flat.","365": "Rent is due, and the Deep City signup window opens the same morning, because the Bureau enjoys a queue with stakes. Sign early. The list fills with citizens who have never once looked down and liked it.","366": "The fog in Sollen's mouth keeps the hours it pleases. If it has not arrived by nine, it is not coming, and neither is the clerk behind it.","368": "When the white moon is new, the Old Ones are nearest, which in Oldber means the fog leans. Do not lean back. It is not affection. It is appraisal.","369": "Other Skin Day. You will be issued a skin at the district office, sized by estimate, and you will wear it with gratitude. Return it by sundown, dry, with the form. What you learn in it is yours to keep and never mention.","370": "The Howling Rites are held outdoors in all weathers. In Oldber the fog howls back, on pitch, which the choirmaster insists is an honor and the neighbors describe differently.","372": "Once a year the Index permits a citizen to be filed under another name for one day, for rest. Choose a name with no enemies. The paperwork follows you home regardless.","373": "The Aria Distribution Allowance is paid at Window 2. Bring two forms: one for the Aria, one for having brought only one form last time.","376": "The Nawn Coalition patrols Oldber on foot, in silence, looking at everything with no eyes. Do not wave. They are working. If one stops outside your house, it is not about you, and the Bureau will confirm this in writing, eventually.","377": "The Elder Raven hears confessions on the 13th. It does not absolve. It files. Citizens report sleeping better anyway, which the Raven accepts as tribute.","380": "Screaming is favorable today provided it is done quietly, indoors, into the approved receptacle. The fog carries sound in Oldber and returns it to the wrong address.","382": "The moon is full and the pamphleteers of Sollen's Mouth hold their monthly meeting, at which they agree on nothing, in writing, beautifully. Their minutes are the fog's favorite literature.","383": "A Repetition Day. By order of the unicorns, today is the 28th again, early: the Turning arrives ahead of itself so citizens may rehearse. What you file today is filed twice. Good luck.","384": "The escorted visit to the Deep City departs at the hour on your ticket and returns one hour later, whatever it felt like. The escort does not answer questions. The escort is the answer.","385": "The Junior High Lock-in & Retreat is held tonight. The Retreat guarantees that 17 of every 19 children will be returned, and 14 of them safely. The other parents have been notified. The tournament runs concurrently; the children are the trophy ceremony.","387": "The Appeals Round opens. Bring your file, your grievance, and your second grievance, the true one. Re-Ratification concludes tomorrow. Decisions are final, retroactively.","390": "Do not visit the nursery today. The Bureau does not elaborate. The Bureau has never elaborated. The one clerk who asked was filed under N.","391": "A good day for Teeth: exchanges are favorable, the rate is kind, and the collectors make house calls. Settle what you owe while settling is a choice.","392": "The Turning. At midnight the year ends and the year begins, and the last form of the year is accepted, or not. Be filed. What is unfiled is late, and what is late is carried, gently, into the new year, where it will be someone else's seniority."};
